{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And he has not washed his hands in water</b> Scripture calls immersion — \"washing in water\" (שְׁטִיפָה בַּמַּיִם), for it says here, \"and any wooden vessel shall be washed (יִשָּׁטֵף) in water,\" and likewise it calls it, regarding garments, laundering, \"and it shall be washed a second time and be clean\" (Leviticus 13:58). The reason is that immersion requires that there be nothing interposing, but rather his entire body must be washed through in water, as in the language \"and it shall be scoured and rinsed (וְשֻׁטַּף) in water\" (Leviticus 6:21), and likewise \"a stream that washes (שׁוֹטֵף)\" (Jeremiah 47:2). And the meaning of the verse is: since it is speaking of touching with the hands, it states that whatever the zav touches with his hands while he has not yet washed them with the washing of his entire body in water — shall be unclean, as if it said, \"and whatever the zav touches with his hands while he has not yet bathed in water on the day of his purification — he shall wash his garments.\" And it was necessary to use the term שְׁטִיפָה (washing/rinsing) in order to teach about the washing it will later mention (Leviticus 15:13), \"and he shall wash his flesh in living water,\" that it must be with rinsing and rubbing to remove any interposition, as I have explained.\n\nAnd it is not correct to say that whatever the zav touches after he washes his hands in water will not become unclean, for Scripture has already stated that whoever touches the flesh of the zav — wherever on him it may be — becomes unclean, and likewise regarding his bed, his seat, and the vessel on which he sits, until he becomes clean of his zav-flow and washes his flesh in living water and is clean. Rather, Scripture alludes to his purification through complete immersion, as I have explained.\n\nAnd our Sages said (Torat Kohanim, Zavim, chapter 4:5) that Scripture expressed the immersion of the zav's body in the language of washing the hands, to teach you that the concealed parts do not require entry into water but only the exposed organ, like the hands.\n\nAnd the reason for the impurity of the zav-flow in a man is that it is a severe illness among the illnesses that cling, and he requires a sacrifice to give acknowledgment (הוֹדָאָה) to HaShem that He healed him and purified him, and he requires a sin-offering to atone for his sin, so that he not cause himself illness again.\n\nAnd the reason for the impurity of a semen emission (Leviticus 15:16–18), even though it is part of the natural order, is like the reason for the impurity of a corpse, for the source is corrupted, and the one who lies with a woman does not know whether his seed will be corrupted or whether from it a child will be formed. And in my mention of the impurity of a corpse by means of the One who brings death and the One who brings life, the impurity of the semen emission will be explained to you. And I will yet mention principles regarding the menstruating woman (Ramban on Leviticus 18:19).\n\nScriptive was lenient regarding a woman's zav-flow at the time of her menstruation and did not obligate her in a sacrifice, because it is in her nature and she is not being healed from an illness, and it rendered her impure for seven days, whether she sees for one day or all seven, for women by their nature do not have more than seven days except when there is an excess of illness. But when her blood-flow continues many days beyond her known time, or she exceeds the usual time and her blood-flow continues many days after those seven, that is an illness like the zav-flow of a man, and it required her to bring a sacrifice upon healing, like the law of the zav.\n\nAnd Scripture did not mention immersion regarding the woman as it mentioned regarding the man's zav-flow and its impurity, for it said at the end (Leviticus 15:13), \"and he shall wash his flesh in living water and be clean,\" and then returned and said regarding the woman (Leviticus 15:19), \"and a woman, when she has a zav-flow\" — like the zav man, her zav-flow shall be blood, not white as in a man. And it mentioned the impurity regarding the menstruant (נִדָּה) and the zavah, and afterward mentioned regarding the zavah (Leviticus 15:28), \"and if she has become clean of her zav-flow\" — as the zav becomes clean of his flow; \"and she shall count for herself seven days\" — as the zav counts; \"and afterward she shall be clean\" — like the purification of the zav. And according to the plain sense, she requires washing in living water like the zav.\n\nBut our Sages were lenient regarding the purification of the zavah, that she becomes clean in the manner of all others who become pure from their impurity, by means of the waters of a mikveh (Tosefta, Megillah 1:11). And their reason is that Scripture did not need to say \"and afterward she shall be clean\" at all, since she is included in the male, for Scripture came only to mention the distinction between the male and the female, that her flow be blood, and to distinguish between the time of her menstruation and beyond her time. Therefore they held that Scripture came to extend her purification, to say \"and afterward she shall be clean\" like all who become pure in the Torah, even without living water."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Scriptive in the penultimate paragraph is a hyper-literal rendering of הִקֵל הַכָּתוּב; the idiom means 'Scripture was lenient.' The phrase בְּזָכְרִי טֻמְאַת הַמֵּת ('And in my mention of the impurity of a corpse') refers forward to Ramban's commentary on Leviticus 18:19, as the text itself signals; the gender of זָכְרִי is unusual and may reflect a manuscript variant or abbreviation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:54:26.792903124Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}